From: PupJune2 <PupJune2@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0000
Subject: CromIKO for Windows: download manager
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The CromIKO download manager of the Android app (CromIKO-Download-
Manager.patch) on the desktop. Its queue is shared C++, so it now runs on
every platform:

- Big files download over several connections at once: parallel
  downloading is turned on, with 4 connections per file by default (1 to
  8).
- Only 3 downloads run at the same time by default (1 to 5, or no
  limit); the others wait in a queue and start when one ends. A waiting
  download that the user resumes starts right away.
- "Pause on metered connections": downloads wait while Windows says the
  connection is metered, and continue on an unmetered one.

The downloads say why they wait instead of "Paused", in chrome://downloads
and in the download bubble: "In queue" or "Waiting for an unmetered
connection". A download that uses more than one connection says how many:
"2.4 MB/s - 12 MB of 300 MB, 2 mins left • 4 connections".

The settings are in Settings > Downloads, in a "CromIKO download manager"
card below Chromium's: a tinted top with the downloads icon of the logo,
"Connections per file" and "Downloads at the same time" with a preview
next to each that follows the choice (one file split into a part for each
connection, a row of files that run or wait), and "Pause on metered
connections". They are the same profile prefs as on Android.

